in a first the ceo of apple testified in  a groundbreaking court case  tim cook was in federal court in  california defending charges  that his company's app store is an  unfair monopoly  at cbs news technology reporter dan  patterson tells us that tech giant is  squaring off against the maker  of a popular video game those apps  populating iphones have been put in the  spotlight  in a federal court case in oakland  california where apple ceo  tim cook was called to defend charges  his company is ripping off the millions  of developers  who make the apps  the charges against apple center on one  app  a video game called fortnite developed  by epic games  epic found a way to bypass the 30  commission  apple collects for app purchases apple  then  booted fortnite from its store in a  lawsuit  epic argues apple's removal of fortnite  is yet another example of apple flexing  its enormous power  to maintain a monopoly a position echoed  by megan dimuzio  who runs the coalition for app fairness  if you want  your app on an iphone or an ipad you  have to go through the app store  period there's no alternative is google  guilty of doing the same thing  there are similar grievances with google  as there are with apple  however you know our developer members  continue to point towards apple as  having the most egregious behavior  in court yesterday cook testified that  the app store is not a monopoly and  forcing apple  to allow competing app stores on the  device would damage the  consumer experience and lead to  increased malware  the phalanx of lawyers for each side  doing battle all this week in oakland  underscore  how high the stakes are
 

